WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.700
Travis Seppala, Screenwriter/Author: Tip #2…

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.500
Film Courage: Okay…so Tip #2 - There are
only two screenwriting rules?

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.950
Travis: Yes, so I’ve got in my book here
[365: A Year Of Screenwriting Tips] I’ve

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:17.240
even got them labeled for you - Rule #1 is
“Write a script that looks like a script.”

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.190
Which I’ve already talked about, right?

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.970
So if it looks like a novel, it’s not a
script.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:23.250
It’s going in the garbage.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.529
So if it looks like a script you’ve at least
gotten past the first game because there are

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:30.180
a lot of scripts that come in that don’t
look like scripts.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:36.750
When I was doing reading for a producer I
was sent the script to read that was three

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.630
hundred and twelve pages.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:41.720
I’m not reading a three hundred and twelve
page script!

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.090
It’s not happening!

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.399
It doesn’t look like a script right off
the bat.

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:50.190
So as long as it looks like a script even
the formatting you can have little tweaks

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.140
here and there as long as it still looks like
a script.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.370
And #2 “Write a script that feels like a
movie.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:57.910
Preferably a good movie.”

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:02.770
If you’re obviously writing TV then it would
be right to have it feel like a TV episode

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:07.890
if you are writing this big lengthy thing
that feels like it should be a novel you’re

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.320
getting into the character’s mind (all this
other stuff).

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.100
It’s not a movie anymore.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.170
If you’re telling me something where it’s
just us sitting here having a conversation,

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.760
this is great for a YouTube video.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.610
I’m not going to watch a whole movie of
me sitting here talking to you.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:25.009
Even documentaries they are cutting away to
other stuff, right?

00:01:25.009 --> 00:01:27.580
Other things are happening than just two people
talking to each other.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:32.689
It needs to look like a script and it needs
to feel like a movie.

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:34.220
Bonus points if it’s a good movie.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.509
Film Courage: I like that.

00:01:35.509 --> 00:01:39.329
So don’t write War And Peace and submit
it.

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:40.329
Travis: Oh yeah.

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:42.759
Film Courage: Don’t have an eight-part series…

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:45.850
Travis: Yes because if it’s a short.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.759
So I’ve been asked the question from people
on Facebook and stuff where they’ve DM’d

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:55.910
me and asked me how can I tell if it’s a
short or a feature or a show?

00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:01.439
If you can tell the entire story in like 20
minutes or less and be done with it, it’s

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:03.119
a short.

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:08.869
If it’s going to take at least a half an
hour to tell it and you can have it done in

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:12.060
like three hours and then that’s it, we
never have to see these characters again,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.060
like it’s a feature.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:17.180
But if it’s too short, flesh it out to feature
to get there.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.760
If it’s three hours, try to pull back a
little bit to make it a feature.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.760
But it’s a feature if you can tell it in
a couple of hours.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:30.730
But if it’s something that could go on and
on forever, it’s probably a television series.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:37.480
If I’m doing a comic book, if I’m doing
the Flash TV series versus the Flash movie

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:45.180
that may or may not even happen now with Ezra
Miller, those are going to be two very different

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.019
Flash stories because in one they are going
to be telling the story between like an hour

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:50.700
and a half or two and a half hours.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.480
The other one we are already like what, six
seasons in and it’s still going?

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.250
We can have episodic and keep going.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:04.560
They are going to be two very different stories
to tell sort of thing.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.470
So that is the thing with it being cinematic
is figuring out where does it fall in line?

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:10.709
Is it a short?

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:12.379
Is it a feature?

00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:13.459
Is it a TV show?

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.500
Is it a book?

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.019
Is it a comic book series?

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:18.349
Is it a poem?

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:27.010
All of these things have different worlds
and guidelines to them but it needs to go

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:28.360
around the story you want to tell.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:32.780
So if the story you want to tell if you could
keep telling it over and over with different

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.420
things, it’s probably a TV series.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:39.250
If you’re going to tell me this succinct
little story that’s cool and has a couple

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:43.470
of different plots (A plot, C plot) but it’s
only going to take you a few hours to tell

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.780
it, it’s a feature.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:52.330
Keeping those in mind is a way to figure out
where your story lies among all of the medium

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.189
that there is to create in.

00:03:55.189 --> 00:03:56.269
It might be a painting.

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:57.349
I don’t know?

00:03:57.349 --> 00:04:02.340
Film Courage: (Reading another part from Travis’s
book) “You cannot trash talk the field you

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.130
want to be a part of.”

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:10.120
Travis: Yes, this is one of the things…so
a big reason I wrote this book to begin with

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:16.340
was because of seeing people on the Facebook
screenwriting groups asking the same questions

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:17.680
over and over.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.461
This particular tip was seen as a huge faux
pas that a lot of writers do where they are

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:26.490
like “I am writing this because there is
NOTHING good in Hollywood!

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.190
There are no good movies anymore.”

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:33.411
If you’re announcing that to the world why
do you want to make movies if there is nothing

00:04:33.411 --> 00:04:34.411
good?

00:04:34.411 --> 00:04:39.110
What is the point of you going into a field
where you think everything out there is garbage?

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.270
Why do you want to be part of this?

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:46.470
And why are you going to tell people that
you think it’s all garbage to begin with?

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:50.130
So don’t trash…if this is the field you
want to be in don’t start trash-talking

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:54.360
other people’s work, don’t trash-talk
other people who are working in the industry

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:59.630
that you may or may not ever work with because
this is a small town and that gets around.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:03.070
Film Courage: So save it for politics?

